--TEST--
a named attribute's name resolves to a shape-typed `type` alias and the payload is checked against it, so a name that is anything else is refused where it is written — `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/an-attribute-is-an-anonymous-object-attached-to-a-declaration
// `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms`: the named form reads like a constructor call deliberately,
// for PHP familiarity, and is not one — `Name` is never a class and never a
// new namespace of attribute kinds. It is a pre-existing `type` alias whose
// right-hand side is a shape, and its whole job is to be the type the
// attached literal is then checked against, by `rule:types/shape-type`'s ordinary width
// subtyping and not by a second rule of its own.
type Route = {path: string, method: string};
type Tagged = {};
type Id = int;

class NotAnAttribute {}

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. An extra field is width subtyping and is
// fine; a bare `#[{...}]` names no shape to satisfy at all; and a named form
// written with no list attaches an empty literal, which satisfies an alias
// declaring no field.
#[Route(path: "/users", method: "GET")]
#[Route(path: "/users/:id", method: "GET", version: 2)]
#[Tagged]
#[{anything: 1, at: "all"}]
class Accepted {
    #[Tagged(extra: true)]
    public string $name = "n";
}

// A name nothing declares is the ordinary `E0303` any unresolvable name is:
// an attribute name is not a second namespace, so there is no "no such
// attribute" of its own to report.
#[Undeclared(path: "/x")]
class RefusedName {
    // A class is the spelling this refusal exists for — it is what PHP would
    // have instantiated, and § 1 gives an attribute nothing to instantiate.
    #[NotAnAttribute(path: "/x")]
    public string $field = "f";

    // An alias is not enough on its own: one for a scalar names no shape for
    // the payload to be checked against either.
    public function show(#[Id(path: "/x")] int $id): int {
        return $id;
    }
}

// And where the name does name a shape, the literal is checked against it —
// a field of the wrong type, and a field the shape declares that the payload
// never wrote, are the two halves of that check.
#[Route(path: 3, method: "GET")]
class WrongFieldType {}

#[Route(path: "/x")]
class MissingField {}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0303]: `Undeclared` is not declared
   --> case.nvs:32:3
%A
error[E0726]: an attribute's name must be a shape-typed `type` alias, and `NotAnAttribute` is not a `type` alias
   --> case.nvs:36:7
%A
error[E0726]: an attribute's name must be a shape-typed `type` alias, and `Id` is a `type` alias for `int`, which is not a shape
   --> case.nvs:41:28
%A
error[E0401]: expected `{method: string, path: string}`, found `{method: string, path: int}`
   --> case.nvs:49:8
%A
error[E0401]: expected `{method: string, path: string}`, found `{path: string}`
   --> case.nvs:52:8
%A
